Design patterns in Java are standard solutions to common software design problems.
They are templates or best practices that can be used to solve typical challenges in object-oriented design.
Design patterns in Java represent reusable solutions to common problems encountered during software development.
They offer established best practices that promote maintainability, flexibility, and scalability in code.
These patterns are broadly categorized into three main types:
A. Creational Design Patterns: Focus on the process of creating objects
B. Structural Design Patterns: Focus on how classes and objects are arranged to create larger, more complex structures
in software development. They help to organize relationships between objects, making systems more flexible, reusable and
maintainable
C. Behavioral Design Patterns: Focus on how objects and classes interact and communicate in software development.
They emphasize the collaboration between objects to effectively accomplish tasks and responsibilities
making the system more manageable and adaptable

Creational:
1. Singleton: Ensures a class has only one instances and provide a global point of access to it
Use Cases:Configuration managers, logging, database connections, caching, file systems

2. Factory: The Factory Design Pattern in Java is a creational design pattern that provides a way to create objects
without specifying the exact class of the object that will be created. Instead of instantiating objects directly using
the new keyword, you use a factory method that returns an object of a common interface or abstract class.
We create object without exposing the creation logic to the client and refer to newly created object using a common interface.

Why Use the Factory Pattern?

To decouple object creation from the client code.
To follow the Open/Closed Principle: You can add new classes without changing existing code.
To handle complex creation logic in a centralized place.

Components of the Factory Pattern

Product Interface: Common interface for all products.
Concrete Products: Different implementations of the product interface.
Factory Class: Has a method that returns instances of different products based on input.

3. Builder: The Builder Design Pattern is a creational design pattern that allows you to construct complex objects step by step.
It separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, allowing the same construction
process to create different representations.

When to Use Builder Pattern:

When an object needs to be created with many optional attributes.
When the object creation involves several steps.
When you want to avoid a telescoping constructor pattern (many constructors with different combinations of parameters).

Structural:

1. Adapter: The Adapter Design Pattern is a structural design pattern that allows objects with
incompatible interfaces to work together. It acts as a bridge between two incompatible interfaces.

Why Use the Adapter Pattern?
Imagine a scenario where you have a legacy system or a third-party API that doesn't match your application's expected
interface. Rewriting the system or the new code isn't feasible. Instead, you use an adapter to make them compatible
without changing their core code.
Think of a power adapter. Your laptop charger has a US plug, but you're in the UK.
The power adapter lets your charger plug fit into a UK socket, adapting one interface (plug) to another (socket).
The Adapter Pattern allows flexibility, promotes code reuse, and supports integration with external or legacy code
with minimal changes.